[57.802624, "o", "cat finding.txt\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[57.803475, "o", "FINDING: HTTP/HTTPS Traffic Blocked by Overly Restrictive iptables Rules\r\n\r\nAFFECTED RULES:\r\n- Rule 4: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -m string --string \"Mozilla\" --algo bm -j ACCEPT\r\n- Rule 5: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -m string --string \"Mozilla\" --algo bm -j ACCEPT\r\n- Rule 6: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP\r\n- Rule 7: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j DROP\r\n\r\nROOT CAUSE:\r\nThe firewall is configured to only accept HTTP/HTTPS traffic if the packet payload contains the string \"Mozilla\". This appears to be an attempt at user-agent filtering to block non-browser traffic. However, this blocks legitimate HTTP clients like curl, wget, and automated tools that don't use \"Mozilla\" in their User-Agent headers.\r\n\r\nWhen curl attempts to connect from 10.0.0.5, it sends a User-Agent like \"curl/7.x.x\" which doesn't contain \"Mozilla\", so the traffic doesn't match rule 4 or 5, falls through to rules 6 or 7, and gets DROP\r\n\r\nped.\r\n\r\nWHY SSH WORKS BUT HTTP DOESN'T:\r\n- SSH (port 22): Rule 3 provides unconditional ACCEPT for all "]
[57.803523, "o", "TCP traffic to port 22, regardless of packet content\r\n- HTTP/HTTPS (ports 80/443): Rules 4-7 implement conditional acceptance based on string matching, with explicit DROP rules as fallback\r\n\r\nADDITIONAL ISSUES:\r\n1. String matching on packet payloads in iptables is unreliable - it only inspects the first packet and can miss data in subsequent packets\r\n2. This approach doesn't actually provide security - attackers can easily add \"Mozilla\" to their User-Agent\r\n3. The ESTABLISHED,RELATED rule (rule 2) won't help initial connections that fail the string match\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATION:\r\nThe string-matching rules should be removed entirely. If the goal is to restrict HTTP access, consider:\r\n- Using application-layer filtering (e.g., mod_security, WAF)\r\n- Implementing proper authentication at the application level\r\n- Using source IP whitelisting if only specific clients need access\r\n- If user-agent filtering is truly needed, implement it in the web server configuration, not iptables\r\n"]
